{#-
  A RANDOM WAIT, in minutes, re-rolled every time the automation runs.

  webCoRE writes "happens daily at {addMinutes('22:00', random(0,90))}" —
  a base time plus a fresh random offset each night. Home Assistant cannot
  put that in the trigger: `at:` accepts only a LIMITED template (no now(),
  no today_at, no state access — HA's own words, measured 2026-08-17: "Use of
  'today_at' is not supported in limited templates"), and worse, it fails
  SILENTLY — the automation still reports `on` while its trigger was never
  set up. It is also evaluated once when the trigger is built, so the
  "random" time would be frozen until the next reload.

  So the automation wakes at the BASE time and waits this long before acting.
  Same result, different mechanism: the action still lands at a fresh random
  time each night, and this template is evaluated per run — verified on the
  bench, three runs of the same automation waiting 7s, 5s and 7s.

  `hi + 1` because webCoRE's random(a,b) includes b, and Jinja's range() does
  not. Dropping that would quietly make the last minute unreachable.

  EDIT THIS if you want a different distribution — a fixed offset, or one
  weighted toward the start of the window.

  Variables: `lo`, `hi` (the inclusive bounds webCoRE asked for).
-#}
{{ '{{ range(' ~ lo ~ ', ' ~ (hi + 1) ~ ')|random }}' }}
